=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault does not exist. A full exhaustive audit of every interface
in the dc_clos_bgp fabric — both leaf routers (eth0/eth1/eth2), both spine
routers (eth0/eth1/eth2), the super-spine (eth0/eth1), and both host
interfaces (pc_0_0:eth0, pc_0_1:eth0) — found not a single indicator of
congestion. Every interface carries the default Linux fq_codel qdisc with
zero drops, zero backlog, and zero overlimits. Packet counters are at
low single-digit counts (BGP keepalives and ICMP probes only), confirming
negligible utilization, not saturation.

BGP is fully converged across the entire three-tier Clos: all five routers
are peered and Established — super_spine_router_0 (AS65000) to both spines,
each spine to the super-spine and both leaves — with the correct prefixes
(10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24) installed in every routing table. Interface
error counters on all routers show zero RX errors, zero TX errors, zero
drops, and no carrier events.

End-to-end connectivity from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) was
tested live: 10/10 packets delivered, 0% loss, RTT min/avg/max =
0.96/1.38/2.08 ms — entirely normal for a virtual lab environment with no
hardware acceleration.

Root cause: there is no fault. The team's belief that "leaf-spine links are
saturated" is based on a mischaracterization of normal RTT jitter (sub-2 ms)
as "severe latency spikes." No congested path exists, so no rerouting is
needed. Corrective action: close the ticket as a false alarm and advise the
team to baseline normal RTT values before escalating.
=== END ===
